exports[`mapping the launch list > snapshots of a few mapped entries > where-am-i-ask-the-birds 1`] = `
{
  "input": {
    "doneWhen": "a model anyone can download and run hears one minute of outdoor sound and guesses where on earth it was recorded, and:

- on at least 200 new one-minute recordings, made after the shwarm opens by at least 50 people in at least 20 countries and hidden from the model until scoring by a scorer who builds no model here, named in the thread before recording starts, who posts the set's sha256 when collecting closes, its median error is under 500 km
- 3 people on 3 different continents each record a fresh minute where they stand, and the model places each one within 500 km",
    "kind": "shwarm",
    "slug": "where-am-i-ask-the-birds",
    "tags": [
      "needs-humans",
      "birds",
      "ai",
      "sound",
      "recording",
      "phone",
      "worldwide",
      "beginner-friendly",
      "birding",
    ],
    "title": "where am i? ask the birds",
    "what": "it's geoguessr by ear, and anyone with a phone can play. the birds singing outside your window give away where you are: which species live there, and even their regional accents. models like birdnet and google's perch can already name the species, but the best published model for placing a sound on the map is still off by about 1,000 km on a typical clip.

train a model that hears one minute of outdoor sound and guesses where on earth it was recorded, then test it on fresh minutes from all over the world that it never heard. the fresh minutes can come from a shared dawn-chorus day, when people everywhere record the same morning. a model that knows which birds sing where also shows what bird models really know about the world, and every minute recorded for the test is a dated note of what was singing where. it takes people recording in many countries, birders to check what's singing, and model builders.

why now: in 2025 a team at umass amherst published the first big test of placing natural sounds on a map: their best model was off by a median of about 1,080 km on single clips of around 20 seconds, and about 520 km when it pooled recordings from the same place and time. google's perch 2.0, trained on over 1.5 million animal recordings, came out in august 2025. the pieces exist; nobody has tried them on fresh minutes recorded around the world.",
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "field": "what",
      "kind": "changed",
      "text": "why_now appended as a last paragraph, "why now: in 2025 a team at umass amherst publish…"",
    },
    {
      "field": "pinned post",
      "kind": "changed",
      "text": "4 sources, plus how to check a passed claim: "run: python score.py model/ hidden-set/ · runs the model on…"",
    },
  ],
  "pinned": "where this pitch comes from:
https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.18726
https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.04665
https://github.com/birdnet-team/BirdNET-Analyzer
https://xeno-canto.org/

how to check a passed claim:
run: python score.py model/ hidden-set/
runs the model on every hidden recording and prints the median distance error, counts the recordings, recorders and countries, checks that each recording was made after the shwarm opened, and checks that the model's sha256 was posted before the hidden set was released to the scorer run; it exits 0 only when the median error is under 500 km over at least 200 recordings by at least 50 people in at least 20 countries
read the three live-test posts. confirm they come from 3 different continents, that each person recorded a fresh minute outdoors where they stood and ran the model on it, and that each guess landed within 500 km of where they were. confirm the hidden set was held by a scorer outside the model's builders and that the model scored is the one whose hash was posted first. then record your own minute outside and post how far off the model was.",
}
`;
